wajo13_q2.solution:
Using the convention \(\overline{a b c}\) to represent the 3-digit number, with digits (in order) \(a, b, c\).<br>Whatever \(a\) and \(b\) are,<br>$$
\begin{aligned}
\overline{4 a b}-400 &amp; =\overline{a b} \\
\therefore 400-\overline{a b 4} &amp; =\overline{a b}.
\end{aligned}
$$<br>So the units digit \(b=6\) and \(a&lt;4\). But \(a=1\) or 2 would give a 3-digit answer, so \(a=3\).<br><b>Alternatively,</b> let \(\overline{a b}=N\). Then<br>$$
\begin{aligned}
400-(N \times 10+4) &amp; =400+N-400 \\
396 &amp; =11 N \\
36 &amp; =N
\end{aligned}
$$<br>Hence, the 2-digit number \(N=\overline{a b}=36\).
